< remote:      https://github.com/tacplus-dev/looper/pull/new/looper/looper-19         remote:  [2026-10-28T11:48:31Z] checkpoint of #19 (a step) pushed to looper/looper-19:  2 files changed, 123 insertions(+)
I made no change and committed nothing, because the retry issue #19 asks for is already in place and working. I commented on #19 with the evidence and labelled it `needs-decision`.

- **The retry already exists.** gitlab.com/tacplus-dev/looper's `.gitlab-ci.yml` has had `retry: {max: 2, when: [runner_system_failure, scheduler_failure, unknown_failure]}` since its first commit (80810cc6, 2026-10-26). It is on `looper-plan`, `looper-port` and every job the plan writes into `looper-jobs.yml`. Both acceptance criteria are already met.
- **All four failed jobs were retried automatically, and each retry succeeded** (GitLab jobs API):

| Failed job | Retry finished |
|---|---|
| looper-scan-fleet-seatrials | 10-27 09:27Z |
| looper-eventbus-1 | 10-27 15:56Z |
| looper-scan-tenantecho | 10-28 09:17Z |
| looper-port | 10-28 11:13Z |

  The failed jobs the issue lists were only the first attempts.
- **What's left for a decision:** the issue lists `stuck_or_timeout_failure` where the file has `unknown_failure`. I recommended not adding it. A looper job can run up to 11h, so two retries after a timeout could keep one issue on the runner for up to 33h. The checkpoint resume already picks that issue up in a later pipeline.
- **Why I stopped:** that file is in another repository, and the job's `GITLAB_TOKEN` is read-only, so I couldn't edit it anyway.

The issue stays open for someone to either close it as already done or make the one-line edit in the three places. I pushed a checkpoint to `looper/looper-19` saying the same.
